{"candidates":[{"file":"app/src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.resetPassword","start_line":384,"end_line":420,"explanation":"I couldn't open the source, so this is inferred from the call graph. resetPassword decodes the token, loads the user, calls verifyAsync with getForgotSecret (lines 400-401), then deletes sessions and calls UsersService.update (lines 415-419). Nothing in that flow invalidates the token after a successful reset. The token stays valid until it expires, so the same link can set another password. A single-use check is missing, such as comparing against a stored token or hash, or a secret that changes once the password changes."},{"file":"app/src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.getForgotSecret","start_line":588,"end_line":597,"explanation":"This probably builds the signing secret for the reset token. If it does not include something that changes on reset, such as the user's current password hash, the signature stays valid after the password is changed. Then the old link still verifies. This is a likely cause, but I haven't seen the code."},{"file":"app/src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.forgotPassword","start_line":319,"end_line":365,"explanation":"This issues the reset token. If it signs a stateless JWT carrying no per-user state, such as a password-hash fingerprint or a stored nonce, nothing can invalidate the token after use."}],"fix":{"file":"app/src/auth/auth.service.ts","description":"Make the reset token single-use. Derive the verification secret from the user's current password hash, for example forgotSecret + user.password, in both forgotPassword (signing) and resetPassword (verifying). Load the user first, then verify with that per-user secret. Once the password is updated, the old token's signature fails. Alternatively, store a reset-token nonce or hash on the user and clear it after a successful reset."}}
